 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_ZydKgKr5dTycvPvp",
   "name": "Intersection of Two Circles",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Intersection of two circles"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is meant by the intersection of two circles?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Two circles may ***intersect*** once (touch), twice (cross), or not at all\n\n  - Touching circles may be referred to as **tangent** to each other\n  \n    - they would have a **common tangent line**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I determine if two circles intersect or not?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Find the distance, $d$, between the centres of the two circles\n\n  - This can be found using Pythagoras' theorem\n  \n    - For centres $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$ and $( x_{2} , y_{2} )$, $d^{2} = ( x_{2} - x_{1} )^{2} + ( y_{2} - y_{1} )^{2}$\n- The radii of the two circles, $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$, where $r_{2}\\geqr_{1}$ are also needed\n- If $r_{2}-r_{1}<d<r_{1}+r_{2}$ then the circles intersect **twice**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If $d=r_{2}-r_{1}$ or $d=r_{1}+r_{2}$ then the circles intersect **once**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If $d>r_{1}+r_{2}$ or $d<r_{2}-r_{1}$ then the circles do **not** intersect"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2023/09/possible-intersections-of-two-circles.png",
   "file": "assets/b0aead71eb85-possible-intersections-of-two-circles.png",
   "alt": "The different cases for two circles intersecting",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Rather than trying to remember those formulae, try to understand the logic behind each situation"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the coordinates of the point(s) of intersection of two circles?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Once it has been determined that the circles do intersect at least once, the following process can be used to determine the coordinates of any intersections\n- **STEP 1** **Rearrange both** circle equations so that one side is **zero**\n- **STEP 2** Put the circle equations equal to each other (i.e. solve simultaneously!)\n- **STEP 3** Expand/rearrange/simplify into a **linear** equation\n\n  - The $x^{2}$ and $y^{2}$ terms will **cancel,** leaving an equation of the form $y=mx+c,x=k$ or $y=k$ (These are 'diagonal line', 'vertical line' and 'horizontal line') The intersection(s) will lie on this line\n- **STEP 4** **Substitute** the **linear** equation into **either** of the **circle** equations Solving this equation will lead to either the $x$-coordinate(s) or $y$-coordinate(s) of the intersection(s)\n- **STEP 5** **Substitute** the $x$ (or $y$) coordinates into either circle equation to find the corresponding $y$ (or $x$) coordinates This step will not be needed in the case of the linear equation being of the form $x=k$ or $y=k$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Even if not given, or asked for, a sketch of the circles can help visualise their positions relative to each other\n\n  - You can then see if your final answers make sense with your sketch"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "a) Determine the number of intersections between the circles with equations $x^{2}+y^{2}=2$ and $( x - 4 )^{2} + y^{2} = 10$.\n\n> $x^{2}+y^{2}=2$* has centre *$( 0 , 0 )$*and radius *$\\sqrt{2}$*.*\n\n> $( x - 4 )^{2} + y^{2} = 10$* has centre *$( 4 , 0 )$*and radius *$\\sqrt{10}$*.*\n\n$\\begin{array}{rcl} d^{2} & = ( 4 - 0 )^{2} + ( 0 - 0 )^{2} \\\\ d = 4 \\end{array}$\n\n> *Using a sketch may help you to 'see' that *$d=4$*.*\n\n$r_{1}=\\sqrt{2},r_{2}=\\sqrt{10}$\n\n> *Compare *$d$* with the sum and difference of *$r_{1}$* and *$r_{2}$*.*\n\n$\\begin{gathered} r_{1} + r_{2} & = 4.576 ... \\\\ r_{2} - r_{1} & = 1.748 ... \\end{gathered}$\n\n$\\begin{gathered} \\therefore r_{2} - r_{1} & < d < r_{1} + r_{2} \\end{gathered}$\n\n**Final answer:** **The circles intersect twice**\n\nb) Determine the coordinates of any intersections between the circles with equations $x^{2}+y^{2}=2$ and $( x - 4 )^{2} + y^{2} = 10$.\n\n> *STEP 1 - Rearrange both equations so zero is on one side*\n\n$\\begin{gathered} x^{2} + y^{2} - 2 & = 0 \\\\ ( x - 4 )^{2} + y^{2} - 10 & = 0 \\end{gathered}$\n\n> *STEP 2 - Put the equations equal to each other*\n\n$x^{2} + y^{2} - 2 = ( x - 4 )^{2} + y^{2} - 10$\n\n> *STEP 3 - Expand and rearrange until in linear form*\n\n$\\begin{gathered} x^{2} + 8 & = x^{2} - 8 x + 16 \\\\ 8 x - 8 & = 0 \\\\ x = 1 \\end{gathered}$\n\n> *STEP 4 - Substitute into either circle equation*\n\n$\\begin{gathered} ( 1 )^{2} + y^{2} & = 2 \\\\ y^{2} & = 1 \\\\ y = 1 , y = - 1 \\end{gathered}$\n\n> *STEP 5 - Not required in this case*\n\n**Final answer:** **The intersections of the two circles have coordinates (1, 1) and (1,-1)**"
  },
